WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:03.660
Doctor Strange is no one without the Ancient
One.

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:08.980
The character is now known for Tilda Swinton's
gender-swapped portrayal in the MCU, but he

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:13.360
has decades of comic-book history that predate
her offbeat incarnation.

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:18.720
A lot of classic comic book heroes aren't
as old as we think they are.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:23.560
Storm, for example, is a huge part of the
X-Men, but she was first introduced over a

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:27.020
decade after the first X-Men comics hit the
stands.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:32.210
It's actually pretty rare that the most iconic
parts of a superhero's world are present at

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:32.780
the start.

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:37.460
The Ancient One, on the other hand, has been
a part of Doctor Strange's story from the

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:38.320
very beginning.

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:43.420
"Thank you, Ancient One, for seeing me."

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:45.000
"You're very welcome."

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:50.700
The Ancient One made his debut in the same
story that introduced the Sorcerer Supreme,

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:58.190
in 1963's Strange Tales #110, when Strange
visits "the Master," who sits alone in a hidden

00:00:58.190 --> 00:01:00.960
temple somewhere in "the remote vastness of
Asia."

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:06.540
Later, he lends Strange some of his power,
and with that, their relationship is established:

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:11.740
old man and young, teacher and pupil, a sorcerer
and his successor.

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:17.540
For a very long time, that first appearance
gave us virtually all we knew about the Ancient

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:23.960
One, but in 1966, Denny O'Neil and Bill Everettl
plumbed the depths of his fathomless soul

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:28.540
in Strange Tales #148's "The Origin of The
Ancient One!"

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:32.020
It turned out that the Ancient One wasn't
born magical.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:37.140
Five hundred years in the past, he was a simple
farmer in the Himalayas, living a peaceful,

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:42.260
idyllic life, but that wasn't enough for the
unnamed youth who would become the Ancient

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:43.900
One, or his friend Kaluu.

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:50.560
Together, they would delve into the supernatural,
one towards enlightenment, the other to domination

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:51.140
and conquest.

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:56.100
The resulting conflict would destroy their
homeland, and five centuries later, they would

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:58.740
meet again to settle their ancient scores.

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:03.360
Fortunately for the world, only one of them
had Doctor Strange on his side.

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:05.260
Can the Ancient One die?

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:10.240
It's a tough question, but we've seen it happen
in the MCU, so what about the comics?

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:14.140
Is this Master of the Mystic Arts truly immortal?

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:19.420
In 1973, we found out once and for all, and
the answer is…sort of?

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:24.640
Death is already a pretty weird concept in
comics, and it only gets weirder if you're

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:28.520
an immortal being whose powers spread beyond
the mortal plane.

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:33.860
The closest the Ancient One has come was a
storyline in the pages of Marvel Premiere

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:39.000
that saw the eldritch being known as Shuma-Gorath
invading the Ancient One's mind.

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:44.770
The Ancient One commands Doctor Strange to
destroy his mentor's mortal form, condemning

00:02:44.770 --> 00:02:49.480
him to death while trapping Shuma-Gorath and
saving the world.

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:54.980
Strange does, but then a spectral version
of the Ancient One informs his grieving student

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:58.960
that he is "more than alive" now that he has
become "one with the universe."

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:04.620
In true comic book fashion, death wasn't the
end for the Ancient One, but his return to

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:07.420
the mortal world was a little rougher than
most.

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:13.620
In a 1977 storyline called "The Return of
the Ancient One," Doctor Strange's all-powerful

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:19.000
mentor does indeed come back to life as a
side-effect of one of Strange's many battles

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:21.600
against unknowable cosmic horrors.

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:23.820
He's not the regular Ancient One, though.

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:30.040
This time, he's back as a down-on-his-luck
alcoholic, using his magic to ensure an ever-flowing

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:31.260
supply of alcohol.

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:36.900
Strange eventually discovered the Ancient
One in an alley, casting petty spells on those

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:42.500
who intrude upon his drunken stupor, yanked
from his eternal rest in a "soul shattering"

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:43.300
transformation.

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:49.720
Disoriented, he took refuge in a mortal shell
and drank to dull the shock of his rude awakening

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:51.200
and maintain his powers.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:55.820
Naturally, Doctor Strange saves the day and
the Ancient One is able to shuffle off the

00:03:55.830 --> 00:03:58.860
mortal coil once more, "to be one with the
universe."

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:04.610
But for a moment, he had to be mortal again,
a despairing, all-too-human mortal without

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:06.860
the power of the cosmos at his fingers.

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:09.420
It's a lesson he and Strange never forget.

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:15.600
Despite the fact that the character has almost
50 years of comics behind him, most people

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:20.120
will know the Ancient One as an ageless Celtic
woman played by Tilda Swinton.

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:25.440
This is not, however, the only time the Ancient
One has made the leap from the page to the

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:25.980
screen.

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:33.180
The first came in 1978, as part of the failed
pilot turned TV movie version of Dr. Strange.

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:38.600
Here, the Ancient One was a bodiless entity
voiced by actor Michael Ansara.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:44.220
The Ancient One next appeared in the 1990s
Spider-Man: The Animated Series, where he

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:48.740
featured briefly in an episode titled, you
guessed it, "Doctor Strange."

00:04:48.980 --> 00:04:55.940
Most recently in animation, he was part of
the 2007 Marvel animated feature Doctor Strange:

00:04:55.940 --> 00:04:56.580
Sorcerer Supreme.

00:04:56.949 --> 00:05:01.759
Perhaps his most notable recent adaptation,
however, comes from his place in the Marvel

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:06.509
video games, particularly 2006's Marvel: Ultimate
Alliance.

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:13.219
He only appears as a fogged image in a mirror
in Strange's Sanctum Sanctorum, but he actually

00:05:13.220 --> 00:05:15.040
speaks with multiple characters.

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:17.180
Too bad for him that one of them is Deadpool.

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:21.460
"Who dares disturb my meditation?"

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:23.180
"Grampy!

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:24.620
Is that you?"

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:29.200
So, we've learned that the Ancient One is
boundlessly powerful, to the point that not

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:30.560
even death has a hold on him.

00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:35.159
We've learned that he's such a huge part of
Stephen Strange's story that when the good

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:40.640
doctor appears in movies, cartoons, or video
games, the Ancient One usually shows up too.

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:44.600
We've learned that his expertise came at a
terrible price he can never truly repay.

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:47.960
"Yes, but you're leaving out the most important
part."

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:49.260
What haven't we learned?

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:50.240
His name.

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:55.120
Despite the many aspects of the Ancient One
that have changed with the times, he's never

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:56.720
been given a name beyond his title.

00:05:57.189 --> 00:06:02.699
The official Marvel website and the comics
refer to him only as "a youth" in the past

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:04.460
and "the Ancient One" in the present.

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:07.160
Whoever he was, he isn't any longer.

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:09.200
And he's pretty okay with that.

